  "text": "orarily, we solve the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Rewrite the equation**:\n   \\[\n   12x^2 - xy - 6y^2 = 0\n   \\]\n   This can be factored as:\n   \\[\n   (4x + 3y)(3x - 2y) = 0\n   \\]\n   This gives us two cases:\n   \\[\n   4x + 3y = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 3x - 2y = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solve the first case**:\n   \\[\n   4x + 3y = 0 \\implies y = -\\frac{4x}{3}\n   \\]\n   For \\( y \\) to be an integer, \\( x \\) must be a multiple of 3. Let \\( x = 3k \\) where \\( k \\) is an integer. Then:\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{4(3k)}{3} = -4k\n   \\]\n   For \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) to be within the range \\([-100, 100]\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   -100 \\leq 3k \\leq 100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -100 \\leq -4k \\leq 100\n   \\]\n   Simplifying these inequalities:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{100}{3} \\leq k \\leq \\frac{100}{3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -\\frac{100}{4} \\leq k \\leq \\frac{100}{4}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -33.33 \\leq k \\leq 33.33 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -25 \\leq k \\leq 25\n   \\]\n   The integer values of \\( k \\) that satisfy both inequalities are from \\(-25\\) to \\(25\\), inclusive. This gives us \\(25 - (-25) + 1 = 51\\) possible values for \\( k \\).\n\n3. **Solve the second case**:\n   \\[\n   3x - 2y = 0 \\implies y = \\frac{3x}{2}\n   \\]\n   For \\( y \\) to be an integer, \\( x \\) must be a multiple of 2. Let \\( x = 2m \\) where \\( m \\) is an integer. Then:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{3(2m)}{2} = 3m\n   \\]\n   For \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) to be within the range \\([-100, 100]\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   -100 \\leq 2m \\leq 100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -100 \\leq 3m \\leq 100\n   \\]\n   Simplifying these inequalities:\n   \\[\n   -50 \\leq m \\leq 50 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -\\frac{100}{3} \\leq m \\leq \\frac{100}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -50 \\leq m \\leq 50 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -33.33 \\leq m \\leq 33.33\n   \\]\n   The integer values of \\( m \\) that satisfy both inequalities are from \\(-33\\) to \\(33\\), inclusive. This gives us \\(33 - (-33) + 1 = 67\\) possible values for \\( m \\).\n\n4. **Combine the solutions**:\n   The total number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) is the sum of the number of solutions from both cases:\n   \\[\n   51 + 67 = 118\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) is \\(\\boxed{118}\\).",
